A||N||7||trigger||how to get fiero on lift||||5||
Z||000000||trigger||08-26-2000||05:08 PM||||I just started transportation class at school and we sometimes get to work on our own cars.  I need to fix an exhaust hanger and I'd like to look at the underside.  You can't do a very good job with the car on jack stands and you laying on your back.  Anyway,  we have the lift that is in the floor and the four arms come out from the middle.  I have an 88 coupe(completely stock underneath including rims).  Will my car clear?  If it will where is the best place to position the arms so that it is stable and so nothing get out of alignment and I don't bend or break anything.  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||08-26-2000||05:27 PM||||Front: Do the lift arms reach the frame just behind the rear of the A-arm, or the cross member.<P>Rear: does the lift reach the cradle? I would use the cradle.<P>   I use these point to lift my 84 se's in my drive way, they seem to work well. though i don't know if it is the right way. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||trigger||08-26-2000||05:53 PM||||Thanks.  I'm not sure if it will reach.  It should though.  He picks up rather large trucks all the time.  Thanks again.<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||1
Z||000003||olympic||08-26-2000||11:14 PM||||  You'll definitely want to place the arms on the frame or cradle.  Otherwise something will get bent.  Watch out for the coolant hoses underneath.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||08-28-2000||09:57 AM||||I would suggest against using the 'edge'. On uni-body cars, the is a pressed metal seam or edge along the side of the car that you can use to lift it, and the fiero appears to have this (at least my 88 formula does) one of the guys at the shop was helping me get it on the lift and I just about tore his head off when he almost cracked all those pannels. I managed to stop him right befor the lift touched the body. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||08-28-2000||03:39 PM||||The problem you will have (at least I did with the lift I used) is that it will be a really tight squeeze. YOu have to place the front arms underneath, and then most the car forward a few feet to fit the back ones under, and then roll it back and position the front ones, etc. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||08-28-2000||03:57 PM||||It will clear. Had my Formula several times on those and the front had lowering springs. As for locations follow Xtreme's. It is pretty obvious what is hard enough & what's not under there.||||reg||1
Z||000007||tgowens||08-29-2000||05:49 PM||||here at the Fiero Shop we position the pads on the ends of the lift arms just ahead of the cradle (you will see two slightly flat spots on the frame) and in the front there are also two flat spots on the frame -- this way it is possible to do anyt bottom end engine work and even drop cradle and engine without having to reposition the lift arms - once we have the car up there is no need to have to drop it again until time to do upper side work......hope this helps||||reg||1
